<Doc
  title="Programming languages and tooling"
  kicker="Reference"
  description="How the OTAKU shapes translate to PHP, C#, Java, C++ and TypeScript, plus the tooling for each enforcement step."
  path="/languages"
>
  <p class="lede">
    The philosophy is language-neutral; the vocabulary is not. Each ecosystem has its
    own name for a value object, its own way to express a collection, and its own tool
    to prove that dependencies point inwards. Five languages are worked through in
    detail; the rules are the same in all of them.
  </p>

  <h2>The same concepts, four vocabularies</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Concept</th><th>PHP</th><th>C#</th><th>Java</th><th>C++</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Value object</td>
          <td><code>final class</code> + private constructor</td>
          <td><code>record</code> or <code>sealed class</code></td>
          <td><code>record</code></td>
          <td><code>class</code> + <code>operator==</code></td>
          <td><code>class</code> + private constructor</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>First-class collection</td>
          <td>class wrapping <code>array</code></td>
          <td>class wrapping <code>IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
          <td>class wrapping <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
          <td>class wrapping <code>std::vector&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
          <td>class wrapping <code>readonly T[]</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Contract</td>
          <td><code>interface</code></td>
          <td><code>interface</code></td>
          <td><code>interface</code></td>
          <td>abstract class, pure virtual</td>
          <td><code>interface</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Domain failure</td>
          <td>exception extending <code>DomainException</code></td>
          <td>exception, or <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code></td>
          <td>unchecked exception</td>
          <td>exception, or <code>std::expected</code></td>
          <td>exception, or a <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code> union</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Package boundary</td>
          <td>Composer package</td>
          <td>NuGet package / project</td>
          <td>Maven module</td>
          <td>CMake target / Conan package</td>
          <td>workspace package (npm/pnpm)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>PHP</h2>

  <p>
    The reference implementation, and the language where OTAKU was first applied.
    Attributes replace annotations; <code>declare(strict_types=1)</code> is mandatory;
    a class never closes the PHP tag.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Value objects:</strong> <code>final class</code>, private constructor, named constructors.</li>
    <li><strong>Collections:</strong> a class with an array field and behaviour — never a bare <code>array</code> in a signature.</li>
    <li><strong>Ports:</strong> interfaces in the domain namespace; adapters in <code>Infrastructure</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Tooling:</strong> PHP_CodeSniffer, PHPStan/Psalm, deptrac, PHPUnit, Infection.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>C#</h2>

  <p>
    C# gives you <code>record</code>, <code>init</code> and <code>required</code>, which
    make value objects almost free. The risk is the opposite of PHP: it is easy to make
    everything a record and lose behaviour.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Value objects:</strong> <code>sealed record</code> with a validating constructor, or a <code>sealed class</code> with <code>IEquatable&lt;T&gt;</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Collections:</strong> wrap <code>IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;</code>; expose behaviour, not <code>IEnumerable</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Ports:</strong> interfaces in the domain project; adapters in an infrastructure project with no reference back.</li>
    <li><strong>Tooling:</strong> Roslyn analyzers, StyleCop, NetArchTest, <code>dotnet test</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Java</h2>

  <p>
    <code>record</code> covers the value object case; the hard part is module
    boundaries, because Maven’s default is a single classpath where everything sees
    everything.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Value objects:</strong> <code>record</code> with a compact constructor that validates.</li>
    <li><strong>Collections:</strong> wrap <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> and never return the internal list.</li>
    <li><strong>Ports:</strong> interfaces in the domain package; ArchUnit enforces that the domain package imports nothing from infrastructure.</li>
    <li><strong>Tooling:</strong> Checkstyle, PMD, ArchUnit, <code>jdeps</code>, JUnit 5.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>C++</h2>

  <p>
    The most honest language about costs, and the strictest about ownership. There is no
    reflection and no framework to hide behind — which makes OTAKU easier to apply and
    harder to fake.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Value objects:</strong> class with private state and <code>operator==</code> defaulted; pass by value or <code>const&amp;</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Collections:</strong> wrap <code>std::vector&lt;T&gt;</code>; expose iterators only if the invariant survives.</li>
    <li><strong>Ports:</strong> abstract class with pure virtual methods and a virtual destructor; no implementation in the header.</li>
    <li><strong>Tooling:</strong> clang-tidy, include-what-you-use, CMake target visibility, sanitizers, <code>std::expected</code> for expected failures.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>TypeScript</h2>

  <p>
    TypeScript is the fifth language of the guide for a practical reason: it is where
    most object-oriented code is written today, and it has everything the philosophy
    needs — classes, interfaces, generics, exceptions and a package manager. The
    language also makes two rules easy to break, so they need attention.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Value objects:</strong> a class with a private constructor and static factory, because <code>readonly</code> alone does not validate.</li>
    <li><strong>Collections:</strong> wrap <code>readonly T[]</code>; never return the internal array.</li>
    <li><strong>Contracts:</strong> <code>interface</code> for ports; keep infrastructure types out of domain signatures.</li>
    <li><strong>Pitfall — structural typing:</strong> two identical shapes are interchangeable. Add a brand (<code>declare const brand: unique symbol</code>) when identity matters.</li>
    <li><strong>Pitfall — <code>any</code>:</strong> it is the local equivalent of <code>mixed</code>. Rule 38 applies: only at explicit serialization boundaries.</li>
    <li><strong>Tooling:</strong> ESLint <code>complexity</code> and <code>max-lines-per-function</code>, <code>tsc --strict</code>, <code>ts-arch</code> for dependency rules, Vitest for tests.</li>
  </ul>

  <Note label="Recommendation">
    <p>
      <strong>Kotlin</strong> and <strong>Swift</strong> inherit the Java and C++
      guidance respectively: same rules, different punctuation. They do not need a
      separate chapter yet — the rules do not change.
    </p>
  </Note>

  <h2>What does not translate</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Annotations/attributes</strong> have no equivalent in C++; in the other three they are infrastructure and belong outside the domain.</li>
    <li><strong>Garbage collection vs ownership</strong> changes how aggregates are copied but not how they are designed.</li>
    <li><strong>Exceptions vs result types</strong> changes the signature of failure, not the rule that failure must be explicit.</li>
    <li><strong>Package managers</strong> differ in how an assembly is declared; the concept of “no logic, only dependencies” does not.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If your language can express a class, an interface and a package, the philosophy
    applies. If it cannot, say so — and do not pretend.
  </p>
</Doc>
